id #edb/ref 17592186295114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362210}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Interval Using the Complement"} :step/id #uuid "cce76bf9-8e8b-5149-b403-6240c6db4325" :step/math-academy-id 451162}] :activity/title "The Binomial Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167043}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120563} :activity/id #uuid "76251565-5039-56a6-8121-2814efe41dc1" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050685 :topic/id #uuid "5ee1f34c-598f-5608-8eb7-1e1ffa254cd0" :topic/math-academy-id 1794} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120563 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120996 :tutorial/content "Consider the curve ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) that's defined parametrically by \n![](images/a7/a74636de2b4b91905353ed8fd16c8af010260757f9cc70df2a24c23aef2ca55e.svg)\n\nThe point ![](images/1a/1aaddf0d4be4d873ff6c0600b0b239e0565a05ca8daaff7ca92bd5c70bebcf03.svg) corresponding to ![](images/40/407ab78b0b33045b1f97fec83d3d172fdbd3ff8eeebf622e2bb2b550e7bb498e.svg) on the curve has coordinates ![](images/51/51c3c69238e5ffe9c22e9a9a50dd89f69899db2c0895c5f39e2d80db74c6b3db.svg) We know that a tangent vector to ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) at ![](images/1a/1aaddf0d4be4d873ff6c0600b0b239e0565a05ca8daaff7ca92bd5c70bebcf03.svg) is given by ![](images/b9/b9445cca9d656f361fdd089d2a38028714c21f8c5379a43731706bea0be49611.svg) as shown below.\n\n![](images/2b/2b4f4dad09042b761fe950c694c8cf645501106cd1d1d9c0f7a78967b224951c.png)\n\nWe compute ![](images/8a/8a5ec088ee8b89b6df1427fe455f10b0fc27842bf9bc4379d6b112daf01ec056.svg) as follows:\n![](images/88/8875ee3591bf9c6ccf0b3df9ca261c38894a134bb8229253145d000e6f6213fd.svg)\n\nSubstituting ![](images/40/407ab78b0b33045b1f97fec83d3d172fdbd3ff8eeebf622e2bb2b550e7bb498e.svg) into the above gives a tangent vector to ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) at ![](images/ed/ed03dfb662276f1766638162385f0cbef419ba1dd3a0ad02c9ee8d8a5d245cc9.svg)\n\n![](images/39/39ccc671a0f1a1b7df98a151741b7e9e46c0ee9cd8bfd858abe9a9ab4372f3d2.svg)\n\nAs we'll see, it's more helpful to work with unit vectors. Notice that ![](images/79/79a59608940ed4a5091b6b372dfd9bbdd186ab6f8afd946be2405dfd93e2b16d.svg) is not a unit vector because\n\n![](images/ae/ae44ee346812a97fc1e8935852d8615a4185458e1445cb39523d3003e26971f8.svg)\n\nHowever, we can create a unit vector, which we'll call ![](images/43/4326a76eb09b3a7888d11df0bb211ef28a4ab22bfd49a19ddcb96c9f9c98067e.svg) by dividing ![](images/79/79a59608940ed4a5091b6b372dfd9bbdd186ab6f8afd946be2405dfd93e2b16d.svg) by its magnitude:\n\n![](images/06/06ecc34ba9087997ec134c79f93d27ea2a0a3895c790ced864111add41ddc311.svg)\n\nIn general, for a vector valued function ![](images/e5/e5ced9a46fee369462905aace2537d978fd1222337b518b7fb49295793c28964.svg) the **unit tangent vector** ![](images/6f/6fd8da4e323d14ce0567026cff08b94311018226dcbd13f96777b640bb281fe9.svg) to the curve traced by ![](images/a0/a06debad6e153cb789c38ba57ea6e2ac7be55483b45b53a576dbbfa19715f637.svg) can be computed using the following formula:\n![](images/eb/eb59ef8c4faab00d31288eb311ed07c7787758c71d1e959ae7eabf198d7019a2.svg)\n\n**Note:** When we take a vector ![](images/77/771507e21f2935d77160257f7d56c3f5fcef1d0a114ac8a38c373c49790dd854.svg) and create a unit vector that's parallel to it, we say that we have **normalized** the vector ![](images/1f/1f3d1a60926323e963d7563529669e5cf8210771c8cbb0379f7a7d90e7c6aeec.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "de55871f-089d-5982-98d6-3ea78f7e3b5d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2364 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3a2275fb-e73e-5073-a025-2bc601b975e5" :step/math-academy-id 10219 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120565}} {:db/id #edb