:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419419}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "70aeaa22-2b90-533e-ab1d-9dbd9f885f48" :question/math-academy-id "q-70807" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(1,-1,1)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\frac{x-2}{-1}=\\frac{y-1}{2}=\\frac{z+1}{1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨1,-1,1⟩.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨-1,2,1⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨-1,2,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,1⟩ & =⟨1,-1,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,1⟩ & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,1⟩ & =-2 \\\\ -x+2y+z & =-2 \\\\ -x+2y+z+2 & =0 \\\\ x-2y-z-2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "4426cd88-0ed0-5a8a-bd40-151d1e9d59dd" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419428}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419425 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66b70f6a-e851-54e5-aa28-b6a025e9ece5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419408}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419408} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f1bb041-bd3b-51ec-95b9-ab27a720aff3" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419416} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0061273-aae7-5a50-ba3b-7dbc44ba608c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419411}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419411} :answer-field/id #uuid "51f6537d-feab-5ce3-9fc6-f89624d037a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419418} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "373d6628-e7af-5c99-8ea7-bda3f45e1294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419407}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419407} :answer-field/id #uuid "c79424ce-ee00-57d0-b195-b7db6dde7351" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "53aad395-ec2e-5a47-9cdb-e404625e4482" :question/math-academy-id "q-330576" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the point $P(7,4,-3)$ and is perpendicular to the line \n$\\frac{x-3}{5}=\\frac{y-2}{4}=\\frac{z+1}{-2}.$\n\n{{field-1}}\n$x\\,+4y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$z\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨7,4,-3⟩.$ From the canonical equation, we find that the line is parallel to the vector $⟨5,4,-2⟩.$ So, the normal vector of the plane is \n\n$$\nn=⟨5,4,-2⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨5,4,-2⟩ & =⟨7,4,-3⟩\\cdot ⟨5,4,-2⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨5,4,-2⟩ & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨5,4,-2⟩ & =57 \\\\ 5x+4y-2z & =57 \\\\ 5x+4y-2z-57 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "62621dfb-b37e-5d44-bdc0-8c2aa74e902d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419426}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419426 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311671 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43ac71e4-1f43-51f8-bfa4-a92855f4700b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309261}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309261} :answer-field/id #uuid "7faaceec-cd8b-568a-858c-271760090279" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311144} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "580b7964-a0b4-59ea-bd21-b56d80484ba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309413}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309413} :answer-field/id #uuid "93f3390e-533a-5d25-83c0-c5daa77f3ccf" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311213} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ccc2dae-6d3d-5c97-a2ff-2660720cb731" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308836}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308836} :answer-field/id #uuid "95d0ab30-64b1-59c5-b1be-e3cbd8256de2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "04886b81-54da-502a-b95c-1502eb418ce2" :question/math-academy-id "q-336241" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $12y-6z-30=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$\n\n$(r-⟨0,0,$\n{{field-1}}\n$⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,$\n{{field-2}}\n,\n{{field-3}}\n$⟩=0$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨0,12,-6⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12y-6z-30 & =0 \\\\ -6z & =-12y+30 \\\\ z & =2y-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $y=0$, we get $z=-5.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,-5)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,-5⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,-5⟩)\\cdot ⟨0,12,-6⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6908c1c1-4988-5f64-8e7b-f1732fcb1a7f"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419427 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "102de1fa-9039-5418-a721-6fc419237fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+5y+8z+9=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419404} {:answer/id #uuid "90536ec6-2590-5af2-a702-9be2e4b1ecdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+8y+5z+6=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419409} {:answer/id #uuid "c7f81252-3be5-5daa-a718-63070020d187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x-5y+8z+7=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419413} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ca9437-ca61-5c50-96e0-f167dfcd51fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+5y-8z+11=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419414} {:answer/id #uuid "d6eccb3f-c3a2-58a8-b47e-683c853ab3af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "x+5y+9z+15=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419415}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419404} :answer-field/id #uuid "34e50549-8287-5dd0-82ee-b77b6017695a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419417}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e08b1f6c-36a3-5d41-8737-49d26e8563c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-128459" :question/problem "Find a Cartesian equation of the plane that passes through the points $A(1,-2,0)$, $P(3,-4,1)$ and $Q(2,1,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "Note that the position vectors of the points $A,P$ and $Q$ are $a=⟨1,-2,0⟩,\\,p=⟨3,-4,1⟩,$ and $q=⟨2,1,-2⟩.$\n\nFirst, using the $3$ points we build $2$ vectors parallel to the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1} & =\\overset{\\to }{AP} \\\\  & =⟨3,-4,1⟩-⟨1,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,-2,1⟩ \\\\ {v}_{2} & =\\overset{\\to }{AQ} \\\\  & =⟨2,1,-2⟩-⟨1,-2,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,3,-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to find the normal vector of the plane. To do that, we use the cross product of ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & ={v}_{1}\\times {v}_{2} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\times [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+5j+8k \\\\  & =⟨1,5,8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the dot product, we can find the equation of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =p\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,5,8⟩ & =⟨3,-4,1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,5,8⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,5,8⟩ & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the Cartesian equation of the plane, we substitute $r=⟨x,y,z⟩$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y,z⟩\\cdot ⟨1,5,8⟩ & =-9 \\\\ x+5y+8z & =-9 \\\\ x+5y+8z+9 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6d308039-35f2-59e5-b262-324c1a8c2bf2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419425}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419428 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262889 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f87268c-9742-5090-b7cb-521a5d8bbe66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,1⟩)\\cdot ⟨2,4,-4⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260021} {:answer/id #uuid "a390ff13-0937-5eb3-b38b-3df5883b8889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,-2⟩)\\cdot ⟨-4,4,-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261300} {:answer/id #uuid "def43d7d-583f-5acb-a4e0-a37562571a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨4,-2,4⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261844} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c13bd5-c05b-5f70-93b5-0ba177da222f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨4,4,-2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262013} {:answer/id #uuid "ffcc7a65-376c-57d0-ac3e-a501019e18ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "(r-⟨0,0,2⟩)\\cdot ⟨4,4,2⟩=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262119}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262013} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b80e560-2ad7-58b7-9b2c-28eb67acbc7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262329}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0f6aca53-4308-58bc-afcc-f2738d69d1e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-128380" :question/problem "Given the Cartesian equation $4x+4y-2z=0$ of the plane $Π$, find an equation of $Π$ in the form $(r-p)\\cdot n=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, from the equation of the plane we get that the normal vector of $Π$ is $n=⟨4,4,-2⟩.$\n\nNow, we solve the Cartesian equation of $Π$ for $z$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+4y-2z & =0 \\\\ 2z & =4x+4y \\\\ z & =2x+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we put, say, $x=0$ and $y=0$, we get $z=0.$ Hence, the point $P(0,0,0)$ lies on $Π.$ Note that the position vector of $P$ is $p=⟨0,0,0⟩.$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-p)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ (r-⟨0,0,0⟩)\\cdot ⟨4,4,-2⟩ & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c018d688-d281-50de-9674-51b7847b124c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186419427}}] :activity/title "The Cartesian Equation of a Plane" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186419402}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163156} :activity/id #uuid "4373edc4-1d40-5e9a-bfe9-ecc0845c2651" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050835 :topic/id #uuid "6a5936fb-0bab-5b84-a342-e13ec8019ebc" :topic/math-academy-id 3048} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163156 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163365 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with supports $S_X$ and $S_Y,$ respectively. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **independent** if, for all $x\\in S_X$ and $y\\in S_Y,$ we have \n\n$$\nP(X=x, Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y).\n$$\n\nMoreover, if $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ and $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal mass functions of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively, then\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x)\\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all $x\\in S_X$ and $y\\in S_Y.$\n\nIf the random variables are not independent, they are **dependent**." :tutorial/id #uuid "ab3a2705-ec4d-5e72-b6dd-553e9cbb6884" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7746 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b566ab50-5b2d-5693-aaa2-b74aeb985d53" :step/math-academy-id 44847 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163159}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163157 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162153 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161713 :question/id #uuid "1fdcc887-4c52-5d91-ab28-63a7dce9fe5a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12462"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "196e7d31-5af8-5c82-b2bd-0cf1ed7415e2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162388} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275118} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383473}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent Given the Joint PMF Only"} :step/id #uuid "85e8d07f-ff5e-5a08-b15e-b9682157622a" :step/math-academy-id 44848 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163158}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163158 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162154 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161714 :question/id #uuid "4e12d6b6-5771-5e54-8f16-7899f82d90bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12463"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "900dcaf8-da39-5654-96cd-6aec89e379c9" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275113} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281557} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383459} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186383582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186410436}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Joint Probabilities"} :step/id #uuid "3bf9c7bd-4281-5c38-aa27-271d95010465" :step/math-academy-id 44851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163159 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162155 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161715 :question/id #uuid "1875d098-95f6-50a7-b069-56865207b3dc" :question/math-academy-id "e-12464"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35de7257-8d35-5f81-89be-c7faa73d6bc3" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048325} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048945}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162457} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162458} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281533} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281683} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284383}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Random Variables are Independent"} :step/id #uuid "b0b7073a-7020-52ef-b9e8-f169d03cd0f4" :step/math-academy-id 44854 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163157}}] :activity/title "Independence of Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162186}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148276} :activity/id #uuid "9b33318d-1bab-5993-91f6-d6c8c1d3d0a0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050991 :topic/id #uuid "7789d7fa-bfec-58c3-afd1-afcff30a8748" :topic/math-academy-id 2539} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148276 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148743 :tutorial/content "Suppose we are given the planes ${\\Pi_{{1}}}: 2x -y+z=0$ and ${\\Pi_{{2}}}: -x + y-z=1.$ First, notice that:\n\n- A normal vector to the plane ${\\Pi_{{1}}}$  is $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 2,\\:-1,\\: 1 \\rangle.$\n\n- A normal vector to the plane ${\\Pi_{{2}}}$  is $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\langle -1,\\: 1,\\: -1 \\rangle.$\n\nIf two distinct planes intersect each other, their intersection will always be a straight line. A direction vector $\\mathbf{v}$ for that line can be found by computing the cross product of the normal vectors of the two planes. \n\n![](images/0c/0cf233086eccae47e640d3599908572e2c3a9996dd84d861e4d659c03108105c.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,-1,\\,1⟩\\times ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =j+k \\\\  & =⟨0,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1e276ddb-1c14-51fd-8261-8c02eb3294f0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3803 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1ae4dc56-971c-585e-ac6e-a1d1927975cc" :step/math-academy-id 21910 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148278}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148277 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147425 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147341 :question/id #uuid "fb408a64-6d03-594d-92bd-b95c2a5c95a8" :question/math-academy-id "e-5917"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0302bad-e981-5aee-a70f-66045e7f798a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049485}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262995} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262998} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338943} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338944} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366212} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400429} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421901} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421902}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Vector Equation of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"} :step/id #uuid "27bcbb90-63f9-5635-829b-a801f3809b3e" :step/math-academy-id 21917 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148280}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148278 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147426 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147342 :question/id #uuid "68b206e1-07ee-5591-ac08-0109cb2063cf" :question/math-academy-id "e-5920"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b5f51ee-5bde-5e39-8d2a-e5e482be4916" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049132} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050830}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148195} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338925} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393126}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Direction Vector of a Line Given as the Intersection of Two Planes"} :step/id #uuid "0ba22f44-3353-5e3e-a64c-1e09fd59d6d9" :step/math-academy-id 21951 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148277}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148279 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147427 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147343 :question/id #uuid "ae2be3b1-50fb-5abe-9f18-002a88f1de61" :question/math-academy-id "e-5921"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262999} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263001} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263002} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263003} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265703} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338951} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413744}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"} :step/id #uuid "e4a1265b-c056-54ef-b3c5-eaaf48e49ec3" :step/math-academy-id 21954 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148281}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148280 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148744 :tutorial/content "In general, given two planes \n\n$$\n{\\Pi_{{1}}}\\mathbin{:} \\quad a_{1}x+b_{1}y + c_{1}z+ d_1=0 \\qquad  \\text{and} \\qquad   {\\Pi_{{2}}}\\mathbin{:} \\quad a_{2}x+b_{2}y + c_{2}z+ d_2=0,\n$$\n\n we have the following cases, regarding the positions of their normal vectors  $\\mathbf{n}_1$ and $\\mathbf{n}_2 \\mathbin{:}$ \n\n- *Case 1*:  If $\\mathbf{n}_1\\nparallel \\mathbf{n}_2$ then ${\\Pi_{{1}}}$   and ${\\Pi_{{2}}}$ intersect on a straight line.\n\n- *Case 2*:  If $\\mathbf{n}_1\\parallel \\mathbf{n}_2$ but the corresponding equations of the planes are not scalar multiples of each other, then the planes are distinct and are parallel to each other.\n\n- *Case 3*:  If the corresponding coefficients of ${\\Pi_{{1}}}$ and ${\\Pi_{{2}}}$ are all proportional, then the planes are coincident." :tutorial/id #uuid "50ffbdb5-c9d0-5815-9b29-b505cde7f0fa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3804 :tutorial/title "Intersection of Two Planes in 3D"} :step/id #uuid "cba5c95d-7e83-5872-ae95-b6832ff486a2" :step/math-academy-id 21955 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148279}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148281 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186148745 :tutorial/content "Consider a line that is given by its vector equation\n![](images/ca/ca43fd13bd65c544d66a4de2801871c2a79087fd960db44696ce6068d30c8377.svg)\n\nSince $y=2$ at every point on the line, the corresponding Cartesian equations of this line are\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-1}2 =z+1, \\quad y=2.\n$$\n\nWe may therefore interpret this line as the intersection of the planes $\\dfrac{x-1}2 =z+1$ and $y=2$.\n\nLet's look at one more example. Given the line \n\n![](images/1a/1a97b5bcff75bc7306d14a8af3fb1f169a2e7bf865be2e7a9c6d2741eecc63a6.svg)\n\nwe can write its Cartesian equation as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x-2}{-5}=\\dfrac{y-1}2 = \\dfrac{z-1}3\n$$\n\nwhich can be further written as the system of two equations:\n\n![](images/1f/1f4cce850809f3fda876dd96e1cf9c65fd873df97bbba44e291b611896c86ca4.svg)\n\nSo, again, our line can be represented as the intersection of the planes $2x+5y-9=0$ and $3y-2z-1=0$." :tutorial/id #uuid "a7303bab-ef2c-5a9b-9caa-dc6fcc7929d2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3817 :tutorial/title "The Cartesian Equation of a Line as the Intersection of Two Planes"} :step/id #uuid "8aec20bb-3034-54e2-b58d-43a8fc04ec0d" :step/math-academy-id 22145}] :activity/title "The Intersection of Two Planes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186147718}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421906} :activity/id #uuid "39b191e6-3a28-55d0-8f36-ad23b66bbcd4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050991 :topic/id #uuid "7789d7fa-bfec-58c3-afd1-afcff30a8748" :topic/math-academy-id 2539} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0adea858-c23c-564c-9506-9e4a60396c0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413738} {:answer/id #uuid "22b5f065-43d8-5efe-9322-eca1974cc3d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413739} {:answer/id #uuid "6d9614ef-645c-5eb3-83fc-f33c2f35aca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413740} {:answer/id #uuid "6dc4adca-656f-5e68-9890-f48be898ac6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413741} {:answer/id #uuid "f5604891-08a1-55fe-b657-ffce04ef2384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413742}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186413741} :answer-field/id #uuid "aef76bea-df64-529b-85e7-fa51fb7e165e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186413743}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "abe7bafd-4069-5c23-9e34-e0cbd57e3732" :question/math-academy-id "q-71465" :question/problem "Given the two planes $x+y+z=0$ and $x+y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\nII. The planes intersect on a line.\n\nIII. The planes are coincident." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x+y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,1,1⟩\\times ⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+j \\\\  & =⟨-1,1,0⟩\\ne ⟨1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. Hence, the corresponding planes must intersect on a line.\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, two non-parallel planes could not be coincident either.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true."} :step/id #uuid "0311a340-7168-5cea-821e-a5c70f05f78d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421907}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262998 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bbb0f75-5837-5fef-9156-1bb0626a6799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-3,\\,b=⟨-1,1,2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260264} {:answer/id #uuid "358aeb92-2af7-5b63-a3fc-98a16739711a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260355} {:answer/id #uuid "4ae1bb4f-6418-5cbe-8748-f187b44dbec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=3,\\,b=⟨-1,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260517} {:answer/id #uuid "83b18a92-b695-5c83-b721-b2dee329f376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=-6,\\,b=⟨-2,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261019} {:answer/id #uuid "db99d772-027a-5c17-b647-51f20577ed39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=5,\\,b=⟨1,2,1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261817}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260517} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e5261fb-a30e-5c21-b272-d448a6432184" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262235}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ad00dc96-c374-5320-be1d-ca73d7e96f79" :question/math-academy-id "q-129238" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $x+y-z-3=0$ and $x+2y-3z+18=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨{a}_{1},{a}_{2},0⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $z=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation gives  \n\n$$\nx+y-0-3=0\\,⟹\\,x=3-y,\n$$\n\nand substituting $z=0$ and $x=3-y$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3-y)+2y-3(0)+18 & =0 \\\\ 3+y+18 & =0 \\\\ y & =-21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=3-y=3+21=24$ and $a=⟨24,-21,0⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+y-z-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,-1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+2y-3z+18=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,2,-3⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,1,-1⟩\\times ⟨1,2,-3⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -3\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+2j+k \\\\  & =⟨-1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨24,-21,0⟩+t⟨-1,2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{2}=24-21=3,$ and $b=⟨-1,2,1⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "4b62f78b-d432-5341-a2aa-4388a4c716e4"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338949 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ce110a-db9f-5911-9515-94540ed6dd91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336542} {:answer/id #uuid "3f8de119-9259-50a9-9782-4e6da9be6936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336631} {:answer/id #uuid "4c1db453-ad2d-5f60-9a15-9c2d0dec589f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336739} {:answer/id #uuid "e8227368-6969-5434-8958-753fda8ed7e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338060} {:answer/id #uuid "f4bff34c-6563-5a40-bee7-7ecfa61e666b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338170}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338170} :answer-field/id #uuid "8415a7da-95f3-5c4b-9085-9ccf7a05fb9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "894f2787-9676-52de-a718-3b82f8fd7a1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-71463" :question/problem "Given the two planes $3x+3y+2z=0$ and $x-y-z=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $⟨1,6,-5⟩.$\n\nII. The planes are parallel.\n\nIII. The planes intersect on a line." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $3x+3y+2z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨3,3,2⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-y-z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-1,-1⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨3,3,2⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i+5j-6k \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,-6⟩\\ne ⟨1,6,-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel. As a result, the corresponding planes are not parallel either.\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement III is true."} :step/id #uuid "8993a320-0fa1-5bea-8343-47ddcfb4405b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421901 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a5b51a0-3005-5ddd-b5ab-b8d3b530fe56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=2,\\,b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421890} {:answer/id #uuid "77d58298-86f5-5da7-a6f5-5aadeb1af6ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=-1,\\,b=⟨1,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421891} {:answer/id #uuid "782abb1a-40ca-50f3-9eb2-460b14bea776" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨1,1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421892} {:answer/id #uuid "8b4a733d-fbda-5f24-ba39-11bd9f15b6aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=1,\\,b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421893} {:answer/id #uuid "d67766c9-e639-50ae-a0fa-fe37c24c18e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=0,\\,b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421896}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421896} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee9b9e23-e285-51ba-8f2e-0f93320571fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "3582cc5d-9e46-5e0a-8d0c-b0e74f8f5924" :question/math-academy-id "q-71349" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $x+y-1=0$ and $x+z+1=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨0,{a}_{2},{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{2}+{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n0+y-1=0\\,⟹\\,y=1,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0+z+1=0\\,⟹\\,z=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨0,1,-1⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $x+y-1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,1,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x+z+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,0,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\times ⟨1,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,1,-1⟩+t⟨1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{2}+{a}_{3}=1+(-1)=0,$ and $b=⟨1,-1,-1⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "b2250ab1-dcbf-5807-a3a8-ee6a40866bf6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421905}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421902 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bc5a66d-b1b1-585a-b0bc-21d19ca71687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=-18,\\,b=⟨3,9,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421889} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3c1355-587b-53a6-a6d7-2db08ae599af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=-20,\\,b=⟨3,-2,-9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421894} {:answer/id #uuid "d155552d-65e9-593d-bf4d-41010a2e061a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=18,\\,b=⟨3,9,-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421895} {:answer/id #uuid "e23fcb96-7207-5675-97d0-1ce03137e6ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=20,\\,b=⟨3,-2,-9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421897} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b80375-370c-5de5-b724-efe10f25c766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=10,\\,b=⟨3,-2,-9⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421898}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421897} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ea7962c-6925-55c2-86b0-52523c308e6b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "caefbcdd-6450-5b4b-b965-772f5b87b02a" :question/math-academy-id "q-129208" :question/problem "The intersection of the planes $2x+3y-3=0$ and $3x+z+19=0$ is given by the line\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨0,{a}_{2},{a}_{3}⟩+tb,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nCalculate ${a}_{2}-{a}_{3},$ and find a possible direction vector $b.$" :question/worked-solution "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $x=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n0+3y-3=0\\,⟹\\,y=1,\n$$\n\nand substituting $x=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0+z+19=0\\,⟹\\,z=-19.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=⟨0,1,-19⟩$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x+3y-3=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨2,3,0⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $3x+z+19=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨3,0,1⟩.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =⟨2,3,0⟩\\times ⟨3,0,1⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =3i-2j-9k \\\\  & =⟨3,-2,-9⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =⟨0,1,-19⟩+t⟨3,-2,-9⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{2}-{a}_{3}=1-(-19)=20,$ and $b=⟨3,-2,-9⟩.$"} :step/id #uuid "c6d8b30b-6627-55c2-a300-1bd06da939e9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421904}}] :activity/title "The Intersection of Two Planes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421888}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162952} :activity/id #uuid "6fbac718-0db1-5d0a-8eac-581af3c3ed39" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051001 :topic/id #uuid "789ccc3c-cca0-5e71-bdb6-b757b2abcfa0" :topic/math-academy-id 3001} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162952 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163262 :tutorial/content "We often want to know if there is a relationship between two random variables. For this reason, we wish to formulate the idea of a joint probability distribution.\n\nTo introduce this concept, suppose the random variable $X$ represents the number of heads obtained when a fair coin is flipped. The support of $X,$ denoted $S_X,$ consists of all possible values of $X$ and in this case is given by\n\n$$\nS_X = \\{0,1\\}.\n$$\n\nNow suppose that the random variable $Y$ represents the result of rolling a fair tetrahedral die. Then the support of $Y,$ denoted $S_Y,$ is\n\n$$\nS_Y = \\{1,2,3,4\\}.\n$$\n\nThe **joint support** of $X$ and $Y,$ denoted $S,$ consists of all possible pairs $(x,y)$ such that $x$ is a possible outcome for $X$ and $y$ is a possible outcome for $Y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0,1), & (0,2), & (0,3), & (0,4) \\\\ (1,1), & (1,2), & (1,3), & (1,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $S_X\\times S_Y$ is the cartesian product of $S_X$ and $S_Y.$\n\nA **joint probability distribution** is a probability distribution over a set of pairs [math](X, Y),[/math] where [math]X[/math] and [math]Y[/math] are both random variables. In this lesson, we will focus on the case where [math]X[/math] and [math]Y[/math] are *discrete* random variables, in which case the joint probability distribution is called a **joint probability mass function**. \n\nLet's assume that the flipping of the coin and rolling of the die are independent. Then, for any $x \\in X$ and any $y \\in Y,$ we have that\n\n$$\nP(X=x\\:\\textrm{and}\\:Y=y) = \\dfrac{1}{2} \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{4} = \\dfrac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe **joint probability mass function** of $X$ and $Y,$ denoted $f(x,y),$ can be represented by the following table:\n\n| $f(x,y)$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ |\n| $X=1$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ | $\\dfrac{1}{8}$ |\n\nSimilar to the case of single random variables, we have\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = P(X=x\\:\\textrm{ and }\\:Y=y).\n$$\n\nGoing forward, we will use the shorthand notation $P(X=x, Y=y)$ to mean $P(X=x\\:\\textrm{ and }\\:Y=y).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "79b99576-5a28-5c03-bc5d-1cc472ef0795" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5348 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9628dc39-75f0-5169-a945-48c68e6e4f31" :step/math-academy-id 33477 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163076}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162982 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162063 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161863 :question/id #uuid "2d698f44-e551-58a2-883b-3a992119bd76" :question/math-academy-id "e-9126"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38398622-ca45-5eb6-a589-96aec162960a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162291} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247167} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275143} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275144} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275145}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Joint Distribution of Two Discrete Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "3e1d6fef-61c1-5a3a-805d-6a020df8bb08" :step/math-academy-id 34016 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162999}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162999 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162073 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161873 :question/id #uuid "f2789f6c-11d5-5132-91b3-be65dac4ce24" :question/math-academy-id "e-9203"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0e20c64-85be-5cb5-acde-17ffd8b6aff5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162292} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247178} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247179} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247181} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247184}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Joint Probability From a Table"} :step/id #uuid "b093ac87-86c2-5281-860f-df1321b270d4" :step/math-academy-id 34339 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163000}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163000 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162074 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161874 :question/id #uuid "01b86f31-f31e-567d-b798-fc0f10a4d0b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9204"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e247e2c9-3e47-5cf4-be8d-f1f22bb30b39" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247200} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247204}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Joint Probability Containing Inequalities From a Table"} :step/id #uuid "657c560d-1a7e-5600-8883-4eaa35b157cd" :step/math-academy-id 34343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163076 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163328 :tutorial/content "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with supports $S_X$ and $S_Y$, respectively. For a function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=P(X=x, Y=y)\n$$\n\n \n\nto be a valid joint probability mass function with joint support $S=S_X\\times S_Y,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $0 \\leq f(x,y) \\leq 1$ for all $(x,y) \\in S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle\\sum\\limits_{(x,y) \\in S} f(x,y)= 1$\n\n- $P\\left((X,Y) \\in A \\right) = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{(x,y) \\in A} f(x,y),$ where $A$ is a subset of $S.$\n\nThere is some nice intuition to these conditions.\n\n- The condition $0 \\leq f(x,y) \\leq 1$ for all $(x,y)\\in S$ states that any possible outcome for $(X,Y)$ must have a probability between $0$ and $1.$\n\n- The condition $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{(x,y) \\in S} f(x,y) = 1$ states that the sum of all the probabilities over all possible values of $X$ and $Y$ must add up to $1.$\n\n- The third condition states that we compute the probability of an event $A$ by adding up the probabilities associated with $A.$\n\nThe joint probability mass function of a discrete random variable with finite support can be represented using a table, as shown below.\n\n|  | $Y=y_1$ | $Y=y_2$ | $\\ldots$ | $Y=y_n$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=x_1$ | $f(x_1,y_1)$ | $f(x_1,y_2)$ | $\\ldots$ | $f(x_1,y_n)$ |\n| $X=x_2$ | $f(x_2,y_1)$ | $f(x_2,y_2)$ | $\\ldots$ | $f(x_2,y_n)$ |\n| $\\ldots$ | $\\ldots$ | $\\ldots$ | $\\ldots$ | $\\ldots$ |\n| $X=x_k$ | $f(x_k,y_1)$ | $f(x_k,y_2)$ | $\\ldots$ | $f(x_k,y_n)$ |\n\nIn the case of two random variables, the joint distribution is sometimes called a **bivariate distribution**.\nHowever, the concept generalizes to any number of random variables, giving a **multivariate distribution**." :tutorial/id #uuid "5f5858f9-f1f3-5d98-b436-9ffd034a3e9d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6284 :tutorial/title "Bivariate and Multivariate Distributions"} :step/id #uuid "2636297d-b114-590c-9454-08ebab970ce2" :step/math-academy-id 38084 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162982}}] :activity/title "Joint Distributions for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162162}]}
